tools/ - dev-only helpers, not part of the scratchy crate build.

Profiling: tools/profile.sh <url|file> = perf + V8 JIT map profile into perf/profile-*/, tools/perf-fold.py
queries it (fold/incl/callers/jscallers); PERF.md "Profiling (perf)".

perf-diff.py: `python3 -I tools/perf-diff.py old.json new.json [--threshold 5]` diffs two --perf-report / perf-suite outputs (PERF.md).

Driving a real scratchy window (click, type, wheel-scroll)
----------------------------------------------------------
1. Build the injector once (own Cargo project; needs no change to the main
   Cargo.toml). Use a target dir outside the worktree if you like:
     (cd tools/inject && cargo build --release)      # -> tools/inject/target/release/inject

2. Start a nested dwl and get its socket name:
     SOCK=$(tools/nest.sh /path/to/scratchpad/nest)  # e.g. wayland-1
   It writes dwl.pid in that dir; kill only that PID when finished
   (never `pkill -f` a pattern that appears in your own command line, and
   never kill the user's scratchy/dwl: `pgrep -fa scratchy` first).
   The window is about 1052x1050. The backend must be `wayland`; headless
   dwl advertises no seat capabilities, so clients get no input.

3. Run scratchy in it (ONE scratchy window at a time, or clicks split):
     WAYLAND_DISPLAY=$SOCK timeout 120 scratchy --socket /run/user/1001/wire.sock <url> &
     WAYLAND_DISPLAY=$SOCK grim shot.png              # then view the PNG

4. Inject input (W H must be the window size, used to scale absolute moves):
     WAYLAND_DISPLAY=$SOCK inject 1052 1050 move:500,500 scroll:5 wait:800
     WAYLAND_DISPLAY=$SOCK inject 1052 1050 move:54,977 click wait:4000
     WAYLAND_DISPLAY=$SOCK inject 1052 1050 key:j key:c-d type::go\ example.org key:ret

   Commands (run in order):
     move:X,Y      absolute pointer move (window pixels)
     click rclick  left / right button press+release (40 ms apart)
     scroll:N      N wheel notches (positive = down)
     key:NAME      one key; NAME is a letter/digit/punctuation (uppercase
                   adds Shift) or esc ret tab space bs colon slash up down
                   left right pgup pgdn home end; prefix c- for Ctrl
     type:TEXT     letters, digits, space . : / - _ = ? & # %
     wait:MS       roundtrip then sleep

Protocols: zwlr_virtual_pointer_v1 and zwp_virtual_keyboard_v1 (both are dwl
globals). keymap.xkb is a pc+us/evdev keymap dumped with xkbcomp, embedded at
build time.

Known quirks:
- Nested dwl delivers a pointer motion to the client only when the next one
  arrives, so a final `move:` used to be lost (QB3b). The injector now
  repeats the last position once before each `wait:` and at exit.
- `key:g key:g` is vim `gg` (QB12). A pending `g` persists across separate
  `inject` runs, so end a test with a non-`g` key if the next run starts with `g`.

Pointer-flood stall harness (QY54a1, R56 section 2 / R57 section 5)
-------------------------------------------------------------------
The nested dwl drops scratchy ("lost the connection to the Wayland compositor") once
~190-225 pointer-motion events sit unread, e.g. while the main thread is stalled.
tools/wl-flood/run.sh reproduces that and prints one line per run:
     (cd tools/inject && cargo build --release)
     SCRATCHY=/path/to/copy-of-scratchy tools/wl-flood/run.sh PAGE HZ SECONDS [RUNS]
  PAGE: busyN (busy.html?ms=N: a timer every 4 s busy-loops N ms), stall_kN (stall.html?k=N:
  8000 rows + N style.width write / offsetHeight read forced layouts every 4 s; k0 = control),
  or any URL/path. HZ: 500 (inject wait:2, ~460 moves/s) or 125 (wait:8). Motion starts 1 s
  after launch and runs SECONDS. Output: `survived` or `lost at T s`, plus the longest redraw gap
  from SCRATCHY_FRAME_STATS. It starts its OWN wire (own socket) and its own nested dwl
  (tools/nest.sh), holds flock /tmp/scratchy-perf.lock per run, and kills only PIDs it started.
  Env: SCRATCHY, INJECT, WIRE, OUT, W, H, PERF_LOCK (see the header of run.sh).
  Expected on main (R56): busy450 and stall_k18 at 500 Hz lost 3/3; stall_k0 and busy300 at
  500 Hz survive 3/3.

Testing with a different system font set (R33)
----------------------------------------------
Tests shape real text through fontconfig, so a test that hard-codes a glyph
width fails on a machine whose `sans-serif` differs. To reproduce, keep the
system fonts (some tests need a distinct monospace) and force the generic:
     mkdir -p $D/fonts $D/cache && cp SomeSans.ttf $D/fonts/
     cat > $D/fonts.conf <<EOF
     <?xml version="1.0"?><!DOCTYPE fontconfig SYSTEM "fonts.dtd">
     <fontconfig><include ignore_missing="yes">/etc/fonts/fonts.conf</include>
     <dir>$D/fonts</dir><cachedir>$D/cache</cachedir>
     <match target="pattern"><test name="family" qual="any"><string>sans-serif</string></test>
     <edit name="family" mode="prepend" binding="strong"><string>Some Sans</string></edit></match>
     </fontconfig>
     EOF
     FONTCONFIG_FILE=$D/fonts.conf fc-match sans-serif     # must name Some Sans
     FONTCONFIG_FILE=$D/fonts.conf cargo test --release
Only the generic families (`new_font_system`) follow `fc-match`; fontdb still
lists everything under /usr/share/fonts.

CI font set (QCI1)
------------------
CI (.github/workflows/ci.yml, ubuntu-latest) installs exactly ONE font package
via apt: `fonts-liberation` (Liberation Sans/Serif/Mono, in
/usr/share/fonts/truetype/liberation). The suite is run with a fontconfig that
exposes only that directory and maps sans-serif/serif/monospace to it. Same
thing locally (the dir may differ, e.g. /usr/share/fonts/liberation-fonts):
     FONTCONFIG_FILE=$(tools/ci-fontconfig.sh /usr/share/fonts/truetype/liberation /tmp/libfonts) \
       cargo test --release --locked
The clippy baseline CI enforces is the number in tools/clippy-baseline.txt. CI pins
the Rust toolchain (rustup 1.96.1 in ci.yml) because the clippy count changes with
the clippy version; bump it deliberately together with the baseline.
